# frozen_string_literal: true

# bin/lib/test_shard.rb — HOW THE RAILS SUITE IS CUT INTO SHARDS.
#
# The whole module is PURE: files in, buckets out. No Rails, no filesystem beyond an
# explicit glob, no network. That is deliberate — the gate that audits this lane
# (bin/rails-executed-set-check) must be able to derive the expected set WITHOUT
# trusting the planner, and a unit test must be able to prove the packing property
# without running a suite.
#
# WHY BIN-PACK RATHER THAN SPLIT ALPHABETICALLY.
# Test files are wildly unequal. Measured on this repo (test/timings.yml, generated by
# `bin/measure-test-timings`), the heaviest single file costs more than the lightest
# two hundred combined. An alphabetical or round-robin cut therefore produces shards
# whose wall-clocks differ by multiples, and a sharded lane is only as fast as its
# slowest shard — so a bad cut throws away most of the parallelism it just paid for.
#
# THE ALGORITHM is longest-processing-time-first (LPT): sort the files by measured cost
# descending, then repeatedly place the next file in the bucket that is currently
# lightest. LPT is the standard greedy approximation for multiway partitioning and is
# guaranteed within 4/3 of optimal — far inside the noise of a CI runner. It is also
# DETERMINISTIC, which matters more here than optimality: the same tree must always
# produce the same cut, or a re-run of a red shard would run a different set of files
# than the run it is re-running.
#
# Ties are broken by path, ascending, and a tie between buckets goes to the lowest
# index. Both are arbitrary; both must be STATED, because "deterministic" is a property
# of the tie-breaks, not of the sort.
#
# A FILE WITH NO MEASUREMENT gets the median of the measured files, not zero. Zero is
# the tempting default and it is the wrong one, in two escalating ways. Mildly: new test
# files are added constantly and are not systematically cheap, so weighting them at zero
# packs them into whichever bucket happens to be lightest — the imbalance this module
# exists to avoid. Catastrophically, when NO file has a measurement (a missing or empty
# test/timings.yml), every weight is zero, every bucket stays at load zero, and the
# lightest-bucket rule keeps choosing index 0: MEASURED, all 459 files landed in shard 1
# and the other three ran nothing. So the fallback floors at 1.0 and the packer degrades
# to an even split BY COUNT rather than to a single bucket. Pinned by
# test/lib/test_shard_test.rb#test_unit_an_empty_timings_file_still_spreads_the_files.
#
# TIMINGS ARE AN OPTIMISATION, NEVER A CONTRACT. Nothing here decides WHETHER a file
# runs — only WHICH shard runs it. Every file matched by the glob lands in exactly one
# bucket whether or not it has a timing, and bin/rails-executed-set-check re-derives the
# expected set from the tree afterwards and refuses a run that lost one. So a corrupt,
# empty or missing test/timings.yml makes the lane SLOWER and cannot make it narrower.

require "yaml"

module TestShard
  module_function

  # Every committed test file this lane owns, repo-relative and sorted.
  #
  # Derived from the contract's globs, NOT from a checked-in list, so adding a test file
  # enrolls it automatically. `exclude` carries the lanes that are somebody else's job
  # (test/system belongs to the `system` job, which needs a browser this lane does not
  # install).
  def candidate_files(root:, include_globs:, exclude_globs: [])
    included = include_globs.flat_map { |glob| Dir.glob(File.join(root, glob)) }
    excluded = exclude_globs.flat_map { |glob| Dir.glob(File.join(root, glob)) }

    (included - excluded)
      .select { |path| File.file?(path) }
      .map { |path| relative(path, root) }
      .uniq
      .sort
  end

  def relative(path, root)
    absolute = File.expand_path(path)
    prefix = File.join(File.expand_path(root), "")
    absolute.start_with?(prefix) ? absolute.delete_prefix(prefix) : absolute
  end

  # files => [String], timings => { "path" => Float }, shards => Integer
  # Returns an Array of `shards` Arrays of paths, each inner array sorted by path.
  #
  # INVARIANT, and the one the unit test pins: the concatenation of the result is a
  # PERMUTATION of `files` — nothing dropped, nothing duplicated, for every input.
  def plan(files:, timings:, shards:)
    raise ArgumentError, "shards must be >= 1 (got #{shards.inspect})" unless shards.is_a?(Integer) && shards >= 1

    fallback = fallback_weight(timings)
    weighted = files.map { |file| [ file, positive(timings[file], fallback) ] }

    # Heaviest first; ties by path so the cut is reproducible.
    weighted.sort_by! { |file, weight| [ -weight, file ] }

    buckets = Array.new(shards) { [] }
    loads = Array.new(shards, 0.0)

    weighted.each do |file, weight|
      lightest = loads.each_with_index.min_by { |load, index| [ load, index ] }.last
      buckets[lightest] << file
      loads[lightest] += weight
    end

    buckets.each(&:sort!)
  end

  # 1-based shard index, as the CI matrix numbers them.
  def files_for(index:, shards:, files:, timings:)
    raise ArgumentError, "shard index must be 1..#{shards} (got #{index.inspect})" unless index.is_a?(Integer) && index >= 1 && index <= shards

    plan(files: files, timings: timings, shards: shards)[index - 1]
  end

  # The projected seconds each bucket carries — what `--explain` prints, and what a
  # reviewer looks at to decide whether the cut is balanced enough to be worth it.
  def loads(buckets, timings)
    fallback = fallback_weight(timings)
    buckets.map { |bucket| bucket.sum { |file| positive(timings[file], fallback) }.round(1) }
  end

  # The weight an unmeasured file carries. The median of what IS measured, floored at
  # 1.0 so the packer can never see an all-zero weight vector (see the header).
  def fallback_weight(timings)
    [ median_weight(timings), 1.0 ].max
  end

  # A measured value only counts when it is positive; a 0.0 in the timings file is a
  # measurement artefact, not a free test, and it would reintroduce the collapse.
  def positive(value, fallback)
    numeric = value.to_f
    numeric.positive? ? numeric : fallback
  end

  def median_weight(timings)
    values = timings.values.map(&:to_f).sort
    return 0.0 if values.empty?

    middle = values.length / 2
    values.length.odd? ? values[middle] : ((values[middle - 1] + values[middle]) / 2.0)
  end

  # test/timings.yml — { "test/models/x_test.rb" => 1.23 }. A missing or unreadable file
  # is NOT an error: the lane still runs every file, just less evenly (see the header).
  def load_timings(path)
    return {} unless path && File.exist?(path)

    parsed = YAML.safe_load_file(path, permitted_classes: [], aliases: false)
    return {} unless parsed.is_a?(Hash)

    parsed.each_with_object({}) do |(file, seconds), acc|
      acc[file.to_s] = seconds.to_f if seconds.is_a?(Numeric)
    end
  rescue StandardError
    {}
  end

  # config/rails_lane.yml — the lane's contract. Read by the planner AND by the gate.
  def load_contract(path)
    parsed = YAML.safe_load_file(path, permitted_classes: [], aliases: false)
    raise ArgumentError, "#{path}: expected a mapping" unless parsed.is_a?(Hash)

    {
      shards: Integer(parsed.fetch("shards")),
      include_globs: Array(parsed.fetch("include")).map(&:to_s),
      exclude_globs: Array(parsed["exclude"]).map(&:to_s),
      max_skips: parsed["max_skips"] && Integer(parsed["max_skips"])
    }
  end
end
